##The output provided is a report generated by Oracle's RMAN (Recovery Manager) command line utility after executing the report schema; command. This report provides a detailed inventory of the physical files that constitute the database schema for a specific Oracle database named ORADB

#It breaks down the database into two main sections: permanent datafiles and temporary files. 
#1. RMAN> report schema; 
#This line shows the command that was issued in the RMAN prompt to generate this information.
#2. Report Header
#Report of database schema for database with db_unique_name ORADB 
#This confirms the command ran successfully and specifies the unique identifier for the database being reported on. 
#3. List of Permanent Datafiles
#This section lists all files that store persistent data (tables, indexes, etc.). Each row describes one datafile using the following columns: 
#File: The unique file number within the database.
#Size(MB): The current allocated size of the datafile in megabytes.
#Tablespace: The logical container within the database that this file belongs to.
#SYSTEM, SYSAUX, UNDOTBS1, USERS: Standard system tablespaces.
#PDB$SEED:... and PDB1:...: Indicate this is an Oracle 12c+ Container Database (CDB) structure. PDB$SEED is a template for creating new pluggable databases (PDBs), and PDB1 is an active, user-defined pluggable database.
#RB segs (Rollback segments): Indicates if the file contains rollback/undo segments. "YES" typically appears for system and undo tablespaces in the root/CDB level or specific PDBs.
#Datafile Name: The full operating system path to the physical file on disk. The paths use Oracle Managed Files (OMF) naming conventions (e.g., o1_mf_...), which automatically generate unique, standardized filenames. 
#4. List of Temporary Files
#This section lists files used for storing transient data, primarily during sort operations or when intermediate results are needed. This data is not persistent across database restarts. 
#File: The unique file number.
#Size(MB): The current size of the temporary file.
#Tablespace: The temporary tablespace it belongs to (TEMP). This also follows the CDB/PDB structure (PDB$SEED:TEMP, PDB1:TEMP).
#Maxsize(MB): The maximum size the file is allowed to grow to (32767 MB is a typical large limit).
#Tempfile Name: The full operating system path to the temporary file on disk. 
#Summary
#The output is essentially an inventory list that a Database Administrator (DBA) would use to verify the structure, size, and physical location of all the underlying operating system files that make up their Oracle multitenant database environment.


 report schema;

Report of database schema for database with db_unique_name ORADB

